Architecture of RMI?
====================
    The architecture of Java RMI (Remote Method Invocation) describes how Java objects in different JVMs communicate over a network as if they were local.

RMI Architecture (Java)
=======================
1. Core Idea
============
    RMI allows a client to invoke methods on a remote object running in another JVM.

2. Main Components
==================

Client Side
===========
    Client Application
    Calls methods on remote objects.
    Stub (Proxy)
    Acts as a local representative of the remote object.
    Converts method calls into network requests.
    RMI Runtime (Client)
    Handles communication and object serialization.

Server Side
===========
    Skeleton (older versions) / RMI Runtime
    Receives requests and dispatches them to the implementation.
    (In modern Java, skeletons are no longer explicitly used.)
    Remote Object (Implementation Class)
    Implements the remote interface.
    Example: HelloImpl
    RMI Runtime (Server)
    Handles incoming requests and responses.

Shared Components
=================
    Remote Interface
    Defines methods that can be called remotely.
    Must extend java.rmi.Remote.
    RMI Registry
    A naming service where remote objects are registered.
    Clients look up objects using a name.

3. Communication Flow
=====================
    Server creates remote object and registers it in RMI Registry
    Client looks up object from registry
    Registry returns stub to client
    Client invokes method on stub
    Stub sends request to RMI runtime
    Request reaches server JVM
    Server runtime calls actual method on remote object
    Result is returned back to client

4. Simple Diagram (Conceptual)
==============================
Client Side                          Server Side
-------------                       ----------------
Client App                          Remote Object
     |                                   ^
     v                                   |
    Stub --------------------------> RMI Runtime
     |                                   ^
     v                                   |
 RMI Runtime  <-------------------->  RMI Runtime
                  Network
           RMI Registry (Naming Service)

5. Key Features
===============
    Uses Java-to-Java communication only
    Uses serialization to pass objects
    Built on top of TCP/IP
    Supports passing objects as parameters

6. Important Classes / Interfaces
=================================
    java.rmi.Remote
    java.rmi.server.UnicastRemoteObject
    java.rmi.Naming
    rmiregistry

7. Advantages
=============
    Simple to use for Java developers
    Object-oriented communication
    Built-in security and serialization

8. CORBA vs RMI (Quick Insight)
===============================
    Feature	                RMI	                  CORBA
    Language	              Java only	            Multi-language
    Interface	              Java interface	      IDL
    Protocol	              JRMP	                IIOP
    Complexity	            Simpler	              More complex

Note: 
====
JRMP: Java Remote Method Protocol
IIOP: Internet Inter-ORB Protocol




